c:client_is_valid.why:
phase-428 W5 (2026-09-06) — ledgering a recorded sweep finding. RANKED-22. A validity predicate that answers false for an entity in a WORKING state, and a name accessor that returns NULL for the same entity.

UPSTREAM'S CONTRACT: `rcl_*_is_valid` is true for any handle that can be used, and `rcl_*_get_service_name` / `_get_topic_name` returns the name for such a handle. The upstream idiom is a guard: `if (!rcl_client_is_valid(&c)) { bail; }`.

OURS: all six predicates test `state == ..._INITIALIZED` EXACTLY, and three entity kinds have further live states the test excludes —
  * client `NROS_CLIENT_STATE_REGISTERED` (2), set by `nros_executor_add_client` (`packages/api/nros-c/src/executor.rs:2410`) — the ORDINARY callback-mode path, not an opt-in mode;
  * client `NROS_CLIENT_STATE_POLLING` (4), set by `nros_client_init_polling`;
  * service `NROS_SERVICE_STATE_POLLING` (3), set by `nros_service_init_polling` (`packages/api/nros-c/src/service.rs:489`);
  * subscription `NROS_SUBSCRIPTION_STATE_POLLING` (3), set by `nros_subscription_init_polling` (`packages/api/nros-c/src/subscription.rs:434`).
Publishers are NOT affected (their state enum has no third live value) and actions ship no `is_valid` at all, so the row is scoped to those three kinds.

HOW A USER FINDS OUT: SILENTLY WRONG, escalating to a crash. The upstream guard rejects a live entity and the ported node bails for no reason; `rcl_client_get_service_name` returning NULL into a `printf("%s")` or `strcmp` is a segfault with no diagnostic. The call compiles and links exactly as upstream's does.

Nothing platform-shaped is at stake — the states are ours and the predicate is one comparison — so the disposition is ADOPT: the predicate must accept every state in which the entity works. Re-verified by hand against the current worktree before this row was written.
